In the United States, the song debuted on the [[Mainstream Top 40 (Pop Songs)|Pop Songs]] chart at number 21, making it the highest-charting debut by a group since [['N Sync]]'s "[[Pop (NSYNC song)|Pop]]" debuted at the same position in June 2001.<ref name="Dials">{{cite magazine|url=http://www.billboard.com/#/column/chartbeat/chart-highlights-radio-dials-in-to-maroon-1006852152.story|title=Chart Highlights: Radio Dials In to Maroon 5's 'Payphone'|date=April 23, 2012|access-date=April 23, 2012|publisher=Prometheus Global Media|magazine=Billboard}}</ref> "Payphone" peaked at number one on the [[Billboard Magazine|''Billboard'']]'s [[Pop Songs]] chart, becoming their fourth number one.<ref name=billboardpopadult>{{cite news|title=Chart Highlights: Maroon 5 Tops Pop Songs, Adult Pop Songs|url=http://www.billboard.com/articles/chartbeat/482206/chart-highlights-maroon-5-tops-pop-songs-adult-pop-songs|access-date=July 3, 2012|newspaper=[[Billboard Magazine|Billboard]]|date=July 2, 2012}}</ref> On Pop Songs, the group made it to back-to-back toppers, as its last entry, "[[Moves Like Jagger]]," featuring [[Christina Aguilera]], reigned for six weeks beginning in October 2011.<ref name=billboardpopadult/> The band had likewise led consecutively with its first two number ones, "[[This Love (Maroon 5 song)|This Love]]" (three weeks) and "[[She Will Be Loved]]" (four) in 2004.<ref name=billboardpopadult/> [[Wiz Khalifa]], featured on the pop edit of "Payphone," scores his first [[Mainstream Top 40 (Pop Songs)|Mainstream Top 40]] number one.<ref name=billboardpopadult/> On the [[Adult Top 40|Adult Pop Songs]] chart, "Payphone" debuted at number 17, the highest-ever debut by a group and the third-highest overall.<ref name="Dials"/> It also topped the [[Adult Pop Songs]], becoming their fifth leader.<ref name=billboardpopadult/> On Adult Pop Songs, Maroon 5 - whose number one ledger on the list includes its four Pop Songs leaders plus 2010's "[[Misery (Maroon 5 song)|Misery]]" - ties [[Nickelback]] for the most commanding titles among groups in the chart's 16-year history.<ref name=billboardpopadult/>

"Payphone" sold 493,000 digital downloads in its first week and subsequently debuted at number one on the [[Hot Digital Songs]] chart, the best sales week ever for a digital song by a group, surpassing [[Black Eyed Peas]]' "[[Boom Boom Pow]]", which topped the chart with sales of 464,000 units in April 2009.<ref>{{cite magazine|last=Caulfield|first=Keith|title=Billboard 200: Lionel Richie Holds Off Jason Mraz, Stays At No. 1|url=http://www.billboard.com/articles/news/490731/billboard-200-lionel-richie-holds-off-jason-mraz-stays-at-no-1|magazine=Billboard|access-date=25 April 2012|date=25 April 2012}}</ref> It is also the eighth-best sales week overall since [[Nielsen SoundScan]] began tracking digital downloads in 2003.<ref name="Hot100">{{cite magazine|last=Trust|first=Gary|title=With No. 3 Debut, Song Sets Mark for Highest Weekly Download Total for a Song by a Group|url=http://www.billboard.com/articles/news/490669/maroon-5s-payphone-charges-onto-hot-100|magazine=Billboard|access-date=25 April 2012|date=25 April 2012}}</ref> The song debuted at number three on the [[Billboard Hot 100|''Billboard'' Hot 100]] on the issue dated May 5, 2012. It failed to reach number one however, peaking at number two on the chart dated May 26, 2012. It would stay there for six non-consecutive weeks, being blocked from the top spot by [[Gotye]]'s "[[Somebody That I Used to Know]]" and [[Carly Rae Jepsen]]'s smash hit of the summer, "[[Call Me Maybe]]". On its 18th week in the top ten (September 1, 2012), "Payphone" dropped to number seven on the ''Billboard'' Hot 100, making it the first time the song was not a part of the top five.<ref name="Hot100"/> It also spent its first 19 weeks in the top ten. Over 5 million copies of the song had been sold in the United States by March 2013,<ref name="yahoo2013">{{cite news | url=http://music.yahoo.com/blogs/chart-watch/week-ending-march-17-2013-songs-great-chart-220437064.html| title=Week Ending March 17, 2013. Songs: The Great Chart Robbery Of 2013 | date=March 20, 2013 |work=Chart Watch | publisher=Yahoo Music | first=Paul | last=Grein | access-date=May 16, 2013}}</ref> and as of June 2014, it has sold 5,510,000 copies in the US.<ref name="us sales">{{cite magazine | url =https://www.billboard.com/pro/ask-billboard-magics-rude-revives-reggae/ | title = Ask Billboard: MAGIC!'s 'Rude' Revives Reggae | author =Trust, Gary|magazine=Billboard|publisher=Prometheus Global Media| date =  June 15, 2014}}</ref>

On the [[Canadian Hot 100]] chart, the song debuted at number 2, for the issue dated May 5, 2012.<ref name=acharts1>{{cite web|title=Canada Singles Top 100 (May 5, 2012) - Music Charts|url=http://acharts.us/canada_singles_top_100/2012/18|work=A-Charts.com|access-date=August 17, 2012}}</ref> The following week, the song fell to number 3,<ref name=acharts2>{{cite web|title=Canada Singles Top 100 (May 12, 2012) - Music Charts|url=http://acharts.us/canada_singles_top_100/2012/19|work=A-Charts.com|access-date=August 17, 2012}}</ref> where it remained for a further week.<ref name=acharts3>{{cite web|title=Canada Singles Top 100 (May 19, 2012) - Music Charts|url=http://acharts.us/canada_singles_top_100/2012/20|work=A-Charts.com|access-date=August 17, 2012}}</ref> The following week, the song climbed to number 2<ref name=acharts4>{{cite web|title=Canada Singles Top 100 (May 26, 2012) - Music Charts|url=http://acharts.us/canada_singles_top_100/2012/21|work=A-Charts.com|access-date=August 17, 2012}}</ref> and on the chart issue dated June 2, 2012, the song topped the Canadian charts.<ref name=acharts5>{{cite web|title=Canada Singles Top 100 (June 2, 2012) - Music Charts|url=http://acharts.us/canada_singles_top_100/2012/22|work=A-Charts.com|access-date=August 17, 2012}}</ref> For the following seven weeks, the song remained at the top,<ref>{{cite web|title=Canada Singles Top 100 (June 9, 2012) - Music Charts|url=http://acharts.us/canada_singles_top_100/2012/23|work=A-Charts.com|access-date=August 17, 2012}}
*{{cite web|title=Canada Singles Top 100 (June 16, 2012) - Music Charts|url=http://acharts.us/canada_singles_top_100/2012/24|work=A-Charts.com|access-date=August 17, 2012}}
*{{cite web|title=Canada Singles Top 100 (June 23, 2012) - Music Charts|url=http://acharts.us/canada_singles_top_100/2012/25|work=A-Charts.com|access-date=August 17, 2012}}
*{{cite web|title=Canada Singles Top 100 (June 30, 2012) - Music Charts|url=http://acharts.us/canada_singles_top_100/2012/26|work=A-Charts.com|access-date=August 17, 2012}}
*{{cite web|title=Canada Singles Top 100 (July 7, 2012) - Music Charts|url=http://acharts.us/canada_singles_top_100/2012/27|work=A-Charts.com|access-date=August 17, 2012}}
*{{cite web|title=Canada Singles Top 100 (July 14, 2012) - Music Charts|url=http://acharts.us/canada_singles_top_100/2012/28|work=A-Charts.com|access-date=August 17, 2012}}
*{{cite web|title=Canada Singles Top 100 (July 21, 2012) - Music Charts|url=http://acharts.us/canada_singles_top_100/2012/29|work=A-Charts.com|access-date=August 17, 2012}}</ref> spending a total of eight weeks at the top.<ref name=acharts12>{{cite web|title=Canada Singles Top 100 (July 21, 2012) - Music Charts|url=http://acharts.us/canada_singles_top_100/2012/29|work=A-Charts.com|access-date=August 17, 2012}}</ref>

In the United Kingdom, "Payphone" debuted at number one on the [[UK Singles Chart]]. It remained at the top of the chart for two non-consecutive weeks and became both Maroon 5's and Wiz Khalifa's first chart-topping song in Britain.<ref name=digitalnews>{{cite news|last=Eames|first=Tom|title=Maroon 5 score first UK No.1 single with 'Payphone'|url=http://www.digitalspy.co.uk/music/news/a389376/maroon-5-score-first-uk-no1-single-with-payphone.html|access-date=July 1, 2012|newspaper=[[Digital Spy]]|date=June 24, 2012}}</ref> It sold over 141,000 copies, which marks the third week in a row that the number one single broke the 100,000 barrier after [[Gary Barlow]] and [[Cheryl Cole]]. According to the [[Official Charts Company]], the song sold 725,000 copies in the United Kingdom in 2012, becoming Britain's ninth best-selling single of that year.<ref name="chartuk">{{cite web |url=http://www.officialcharts.com/chart-news/the-official-top-40-biggest-selling-singles-of-2012-revealed-1784/ |title=The Official Top 40 Biggest Selling Singles Of 2012 revealed! |author=Dan Lane |publisher=Official Charts Company |date=January 2, 2013|access-date=January 5, 2013}}</ref> "Payphone" had sold 68,000 digital copies in Italy.<ref>[http://www.it-charts.it/DL/DL-2012.htm Italian Charts] ''Italian Charts''. Retrieved 2012-08-17</ref>

